<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Second Spanish Republic</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1931">1931</a>–<a href="/years/1939">1939</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Spanish Republic, Segunda República Española</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Second Spanish Republic was the democratic government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> from 1931 to 1939, proclaimed on 14 April 1931 after the deposition of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-xiii">Alfonso XIII</a>. Under Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-azana">Manuel Azaña</a> religious orders lost control of schools, a moderate agrarian reform was carried out and Catalonia gained home rule. After the CEDA won the 1933 election, clashes between left and right grew, and an uprising in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a> in 1934 was crushed. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/popular-front-chile">Popular Front</a> won in February 1936, and in July an army uprising began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-civil-war">Spanish Civil War</a>, in which an estimated half a million people died. The Republic surrendered to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-franco">Francisco Franco</a>'s Nationalists on 1 April 1939.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>On 28 January 1930 the military <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> of General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-primo-de-rivera">Miguel Primo de Rivera</a>, in power since September 1923, was overthrown. Republican factions of many backgrounds then joined in the Pact of San Sebastián, which committed them to ending the monarchy. Municipal elections on 12 April 1931 produced a landslide for the republicans; two days later the Republic was proclaimed and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-xiii">Alfonso XIII</a> went into exile. A provisional government took office under Niceto Alcalá-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamora">Zamora</a>. The constitution that came into force in December 1931 guaranteed freedom of speech and association, allowed divorce, extended the vote to women in 1933 and ended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>'s status as the state religion.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Under Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-azana">Manuel Azaña</a> the Reformist Biennium brought a ban on religious orders running schools, a school-building project, a moderate agrarian reform and home rule for Catalonia. After the right's victory in November 1933, the so-called black biennium saw state repression of the left and violent clashes. The CEDA, led by Gil Robles, won a plurality but was kept out of cabinet for nearly a year. In October 1934 a general strike became an uprising in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a>, crushed by the navy and by the army using Moorish colonial troops.</p><p>The Popular Front won the election of 16 February 1936 with 263 deputies against 156 for the right, though Stanley G. Payne and other scholars hold that it was rigged, which others contest. On 13 July 1936 the monarchist leader José Calvo Sotelo was shot dead. On 17 July the army rose in Spanish Morocco. Germany and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> aided the rebels, the USSR, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> and the International Brigades the Republic, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> pursued non-intervention. The Battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebro-river">Ebro</a> in 1938 failed, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> fell in early 1939 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> in March.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>An estimated half a million people died in the war. The Republican government survived in exile and kept an embassy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a> until 1976; it formally dissolved the following year, after democracy was restored.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Second Spanish Republic” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1935 the Falangists wanted a stirring song of their own to answer the &quot;Himno de Riego&quot;, the official anthem of the <b>Second Spanish Republic</b>, and &quot;A las Barricadas&quot;, a very popular anarchist song.</blockquote><a href="/songs/cara-al-sol">Cara al Sol</a></li><li><blockquote>By the time of the <b>Second Spanish Republic</b> (1931–1939) it had regained its popularity and became the chief musical symbol of the government.</blockquote><a href="/songs/himno-de-riego">Himno de Riego</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Second Spanish Republic</b> used the Himno de Riego instead.</blockquote><a href="/songs/marcha-real">Marcha Real</a></li></ul></section>
